you> /notes lean operations waste
quill> Waste is the gap between your actual process and the "ideal"—which is synchronized, defect-free, and operates at the lowest possible cost [1]. The core goal is to eliminate any activity that doesn't add value, especially since studies show as much as 95% of throughput time can just be adding cost [4].

You tackle it in a continuous cycle: define the variation (waste, variability, or inflexibility), increase its visibility (via Jidoka or visual management), and then reduce it [3].

The specific types of waste to look out for are [3]:
* Inventory
* Talent
* Waiting
* Motion
* Defects
* Transportation
* Overprocessing
* Overproduction
